If someone lands on the site for 7 seconds, they should feel:
“These people are serious. I can trust them with important work.”
That’s the bar.
What your site NEEDS vs DOES NOT NEED
Needs
✅ Crystal-clear positioning (what you do, who it’s for, why you’re credible)
✅ Mobile-first UX (thumb-reachable, fast, zero clutter)
✅ Case studies that prove outcomes, not storytelling fluff
✅ Subtle glassmorphism + Liquid Glass (used sparingly, not everywhere)
✅ Tight typography system
✅ Robust end-to-end user flow (landing → credibility → proof → contact)
Does NOT need
❌ Carousels
❌ Excess animations
❌ Marketing buzzwords
❌ Too many colors
❌ Overdesigned cards
❌ Long paragraphs
❌ “About us” fluff that says nothing
Apple rule: If it doesn’t improve comprehension, remove it.
Visual & UI Direction (non-negotiable)
Design Language
Background: soft off-white / very light gray (never pure white)
Glass: used only for containers that group meaning (case cards, nav)
Blur: subtle (8–16px), never frosted-overkill
Borders: 1px hairline, low-contrast
Shadows: extremely soft, barely noticeable
Typography
System-first:
-apple-system, SF Pro, fallback to Inter
Font sizes:
Hero: confident but not huge
Body: readable, airy
Line height matters more than font size
